Original commit body from ee37d6be67014032f23d753bf4fe2747c5c2ec04:

verify_claims already discards test/fixture/migration-SOURCED taint flows by
default (include_non_production=False, WI-bifob): a fixture reaching a real
primitive is a fixture doing its job, not the shipped application doing it.
The language census that decides whether ANY claim may be confirmed asked no
such question -- it counted every `calls` edge in the tree. So one fixture file
in a language with no taint catalogue blocked every claim in the repo, while a
flow out of that same file was being discarded as non-production. One tool, two
answers about whether a fixture counts.

MEASURED on hypergumbo's own self-proof (live run, not the cached --input the
original repro used):

  hypergumbo verify-claims . --claims docs/hypergumbo.claims.yaml
    before: 18/18 inconclusive, rc 2, blocked by (bash, csharp, solidity)
    after:  18/18 inconclusive, rc 2, blocked by (bash)

THIS NARROWS THE RESIDUAL; IT DOES NOT CLOSE INV-dabuf, and the item stays
violated. The file census is why, and it also corrects the item's own premise:
INV-dabuf and the lab notebook both recorded that all three blocker languages
were test fixtures, so a production filter would close it. Checked file by file:

  csharp    3 files, ALL under tests/fixtures  -> dropped, correctly
  solidity  1 file,  under tests/fixtures      -> dropped, correctly
  bash      44 files, MOSTLY REAL              -> still blocks, correctly
            (scripts/lib/github-api.sh, .githooks/*, .agent/hooks/*) --
            scripts that curl the GitHub API, git push, and rm

What the change buys is an honest reason: the previous one invited a reader to
believe three catalogues were missing when two of the three were fixture-only
noise.

NO RULING REQUIRED, because this is parity with a decision already taken rather
than a new judgment. The classifier is IMPORTED, not re-derived --
_source_scope's body became verify_claims.symbol_source_scope, consumed by both
the flow filter and the census -- and --include-non-production-sources widens
BOTH, so the two cannot disagree in either direction.

SCOPED ONCE, BOTH CHECKS, and this was caught mid-fix rather than reasoned:
narrowing only the language census left the verdict blocking anyway, because
_taint_blind_reason's SECOND check (compute_boundary_coverage) still saw the
fixture's edges. That is INV-motos exactly -- sharing a predicate is not enough
when the callers run it over different populations -- so the narrowing is
applied to the EDGE LIST and both checks now walk one population.
compute_boundary_coverage itself is untouched (only what this call site hands
it), so BOUNDARY claim verdicts are unaffected.

EXPLICITLY NOT TAKEN, and filed as WI-rusil instead: INV-dabuf also lists
"scope the gate to languages REACHABLE from the claim's declared sources" as
cheap. As stated it fails open -- reachability would be computed by the same
analysis whose completeness is in question, and a python -> bash crossing is a
SUBPROCESS BOUNDARY with no edge into the bash file's symbols, so "bash is
unreachable from this claim" would be true by construction for every claim,
always. The gate would go vacuous for exactly the language it now blocks on.
A sounder variant (scope by the claim's DECLARED SOURCES, which are all python
cmd_* handlers here) is recorded there with the three controls it must pass,
because weakening a safety gate belongs in its own revertable PR.

Ref: INV-dabuf (narrowed, still violated), WI-rusil (new).
